# THIS IS AN EXAMPLE TEST SUITE. IT IS NOT MEANT TO BE COMPREHENSIVE, BUT RATHER TO SHOWCASE FEATURES.

"""We can run one functional test."""
simple("test/ok/ok0000_emptyFile.fram")

"""Similarly for tests run line by line."""
repl_tests("test/repl/repl_test.fram", flags='')

"""
Or we can run all tests in a directory:
"""
simple_run_tests("test/ok/*.fram")

"""
We can also specify flags:
"""
simple_run_tests("test/ok/*.fram", flags='-no-prelude -no-stdlib')
simple_run_tests("test/stdlib/*.fram", flags='')


"""
We can also define some helper functions to run specific sets of tests, 
with specific flags, for example:
"""

def simple_examples():
    simple("examples/Tick.fram")
    simple("examples/LWT_lexical.fram")
    simple("examples/Prolog.fram")
    simple("examples/Pythagorean.fram")
    simple("examples/Modules/Main.fram")

with_flags("", simple_examples)

"""
If we wish to run tests that are expected to fail, we can do it like this:
"""
def simple_error_tests():
    for file in glob("test/err/*.fram"):
        exit_code(1, file)

with_flags("-no-prelude -no-stdlib -no-error-context", simple_error_tests)

"""
Of course, a test suite is just a Python file, so we can do anything we want in it, 
including additional prints.
"""
print("--- RUNNING REPL TESTS ---")
repl_tests("test/repl/*.fram", flags='')
repl_tests("test/ok/*.fram", flags='-no-prelude -no-stdlib')
print("--- REPL TESTS DONE ---")

"""
REPL tests are run line by line, so we can have multiple tests in a single file, and they will be run sequentially.
We should write expectations for each test, and if any of them fails, 
we will get a report with the file, the command that failed, and the failure message.

For example, if we have a test file with the following content:

let f (x : Unit -> Unit) = 
    x () ;;
# @stderr-empty
f () ;;
# @stderr: error

Then we will run two tests: the first one checks that the definition of `f` does not produce any error, 
